#[doc("""
Dropping repeats from a list in O(n log n).

The standard library's `uniq` has only equality to go on and searches what
it has kept for every element: the square of the list. What the kaikai side
deduplicates is ordered, so a set does it instead.

A module of its own because importing a set brings its `filter` and
`contains` with it, over the lists' own.
""")]

import collections.set

#[doc("These values with repeats dropped, each where it first appears. Not for records: a set cannot order them.")]
pub fn distinct(xs: [a]) : [a] = set.to_list(set.from(xs))
